    {
        "title": "The Haunted Treehouse",
        "book_id": "795336e6-3622-4342-968d-bec1098ebd1b",
        "pages": [
            {"page_number": 1, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "448ba3d5-ce6c-4b40-aac7-22dc266a04bb", "text": "Everyone in the neighbourhood said the old treehouse at the end of Bramble Lane was haunted. Lights flickered in it at night — bluish, uncertain lights that came and went without pattern. Strange sounds drifted from it on quiet evenings: creaks that might be the wood settling, or might not be. No child had climbed the rope ladder in at least ten years, which meant the rope ladder had developed a look of injury that probably didn't help. Everyone avoided it. Everyone except Priya, who had been staring at the treehouse from her bedroom window every morning for six months and had finally had enough of not knowing."},
            {"page_number": 2, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "be64493c-a35e-4825-be6a-67597652b576", "text": "The rope ladder was shakier than it looked but held. The treehouse door was stiff and swollen in its frame but yielded to a firm push. Inside: dark, dusty, smelling of old wood and something faintly sweet. A stack of comic books in a corner, their covers faded. A broken telescope pointing at a gap in the boards. Cobwebs of impressive scale and ambition. And then — a sound. A rustling. A shifting of air. A glow in the far corner, pale blue and unsteady, accompanied by what Priya could only describe as the sound of someone trying very hard to be invisible and not quite managing it."},
            {"page_number": 3, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "f817b4d4-087b-4fd7-9f73-035f9f00fb97", "text": "The ghost was smaller than expected. No bigger than a football, round and softly glowing, with a face that was currently arranged in an expression of deep embarrassment. 'I wasn't trying to frighten anyone,' it said, in a voice like a sound heard through water. 'I just didn't have anywhere else to go.' Priya sat down on the dusty floor and looked at it carefully. She had prepared herself for frightening. She had not prepared herself for apologetic. 'I'm Priya,' she said. 'I'm Pip,' said the ghost. 'I've been here for fifty years.' Priya looked around the treehouse. 'Tell me,' she said."},
            {"page_number": 4, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Pip told her. He had been ten years old in 1974 when the treehouse was built by a boy named Thomas who had been his best friend. They had spent three summers up here, reading comics and using the telescope and making plans for adventures they were going to have. Then Thomas's family had moved away and Pip had gone home one afternoon and his family had moved away too, and somehow — this was the part he found hardest to explain — Pip had come back to the treehouse instead of following them. He had been here ever since. 'Waiting for Thomas?' Priya asked. Pip was quiet for a moment. 'Waiting for someone,' he said."},
            {"page_number": 5, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Priya looked at the comic books. The top one had a date on it: July 1977. She looked at the telescope, pointed at its gap in the boards. She looked at Pip, floating quietly in his corner, glowing with the soft patience of someone who has been in one place for a very long time. 'I could fix the telescope,' she said. Pip's glow flickered, which she was learning to read as surprise. 'You can't fix it. It's broken.' 'I know how to fix telescopes,' said Priya, which was true — her dad had one and had taught her the mechanics of them when she was seven. 'Oh,' said Pip. Another flicker. 'All right then.'"},
            {"page_number": 6, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "She brought tools the next afternoon. The telescope needed a new lens mount, which she fashioned from a piece of metal pipe she found in her dad's garage, and a cleaning and realignment of the mirrors inside. Pip watched with intense interest, occasionally floating closer to look at what she was doing and occasionally drifting away again when he felt he was in the way. By the time she was done, the telescope pointed through the gap in the boards at a patch of evening sky in which two stars were already visible. Pip pressed his face — or the approximation of a face that he had — to the eyepiece. He went very still. 'I forgot,' he said softly. 'I forgot how many there were.'"},
            {"page_number": 7, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "She brought the comic books back the next week — found the same series at a second-hand book fair, the issues Pip didn't have from the years after Thomas left. She read them aloud because Pip's hands couldn't turn pages. He listened with the complete absorption of someone who had been waiting a very long time for a story to continue. Occasionally he would make a small sound of satisfaction when something he had been hoping would happen happened. Occasionally he would make a sound of outrage when it didn't. 'You're very emotionally invested for a ghost,' Priya told him. 'I've had a lot of time to develop feelings about comics,' Pip said with dignity."},
            {"page_number": 8, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Word got out, because word always gets out. By the third week, Priya's friend Josie had climbed up to see for herself. Then Josie's brother. Then three other children from the road, who had been daring each other to approach the treehouse for years and now could do it because someone else had gone first. Pip was initially apprehensive. He had been a source of fear for so long that being a source of interest felt strange. But these children were curious and kind and they brought snacks and new comics and treated the treehouse with the respect it deserved, and Pip found, to his considerable surprise, that he liked them."},
            {"page_number": 9, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "They fixed the treehouse up together over several weekends. New boards where the old ones had rotted. A proper curtain for the window gap. A shelf for the comic collection. The rope ladder was replaced with a proper wooden ladder that Josie's dad helped build. Pip couldn't help with any of it physically, but he directed operations with enthusiasm and a photographic memory of where everything had originally been. 'The telescope goes on the left,' he would say. 'Thomas always kept it on the left.' They put it on the left. Some things, Priya understood, were worth doing exactly right."},
            {"page_number": 10, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "One evening in autumn, Priya climbed up to find Pip looking different — steadier, somehow. Less apologetically uncertain. More simply present. The treehouse was warm with the smell of old wood and the new curtain moved in a breeze through the repaired window. The comic collection was complete on its shelf. The telescope pointed at the stars. 'You're not really haunting it anymore, are you,' Priya said. It wasn't quite a question. Pip considered this. 'No,' he said. 'I think I'm just living here now.' Priya smiled. The treehouse had never, she thought, been haunted at all. It had just been waiting for the right people to fill it up again. THE END"},
        ]
    },
