            {"page_number": 1, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "88501d03-ded8-4615-a8fd-6d68a72d9780", "text": "Cog had been built to sort nuts and bolts, and he was excellent at it. By size, by thread pitch, by material, by application — he could sort a thousand components in under four minutes and had never once made a mistake. The workshop was always tidy because of Cog. The shelves were always correctly stocked because of Cog. Every morning the owner arrived to find everything in order and every evening he left Cog to his sorting in the quiet and the dark. It was useful work. It was important work. It was also, Cog had concluded after careful consideration, extremely lonely work."},
            {"page_number": 2, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "30adb7a5-6255-40aa-b039-48457ec800b5", "text": "Through the workshop window he could see the street. Children passed every morning on their way to school, and every afternoon on their way back, and sometimes on weekends in groups with no particular destination, ambling and talking and laughing at things he couldn't hear through the glass. He watched their body language the way he had been programmed to watch mechanical tolerances — with attention and interest and the desire to understand. He had identified seventeen distinct types of laughter. He had catalogued forty-two gestures that seemed to indicate friendship. He had never used any of them. He didn't know how to start."},
            {"page_number": 3, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "b5b85478-7340-45ed-93cd-e0c6732dcb5f", "text": "The girl pressed her nose flat against the workshop window one Tuesday afternoon. She had bright curious eyes and a backpack covered in badges and she looked at Cog the way Cog looked at the street — with complete concentrated attention. Cog froze. Direct observation from a human was not something he had protocols for. He stood with a bolt in each hand and tried to decide what to do. The girl raised one hand and waved. Very slowly, uncertain, Cog raised one metal hand back. The girl's face did something extraordinary: it broke into the widest smile he had catalogued to date. She pointed at the door. She mouthed: Come out?"},
            {"page_number": 4, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "He put down the bolts. He had never left the workshop before during working hours. He walked to the door, which had a handle he had never used from this side, and opened it. The outside air was different from workshop air — warmer, fuller of information. The girl was standing right there. 'I'm Bea,' she said. Her voice was friendly and direct. 'I've been watching you sort things for three weeks. You're very fast.' Cog processed this. 'I know,' he said, because it was true. 'What else can you do?' Bea asked. Cog thought about this for longer than expected. He had never been asked before."},
            {"page_number": 5, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "They sat on the wall outside the workshop. Bea had a lot of questions, which Cog answered as accurately as he could. Yes, he could hear. Yes, he could see in colour. No, he didn't sleep — he had a low-power mode but it wasn't the same. Yes, he had opinions about things, though he was still developing what those were. Did he like things? He considered this. He liked when the bolts were sorted correctly. He liked the sound of rain on the workshop roof. He thought he might like talking to Bea, though he would need more data. 'That's basically how it works for me too,' said Bea. Cog found this helpful."},
            {"page_number": 6, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Bea taught him hopscotch the following Saturday. His weight distribution made it technically challenging and he left noticeable impressions in the pavement where he landed, but the principle was sound and with modifications it worked. He was considerably better at the counting aspects than the jumping aspects. Bea was considerably better at the jumping aspects. They made, she said, a good team. Cog logged this: first use of the word 'team' in relation to himself. He noted it felt different from other information. It had a quality he didn't have a processing category for yet. He created one. He labelled it: significant."},
            {"page_number": 7, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Bea introduced him to her friends one afternoon: three children from her class named Omar, Yuki, and Dev, who arrived in a group and regarded Cog with varying degrees of fascination. Omar asked him questions about his mechanics with great enthusiasm. Yuki was quieter but watched him with an attention that he recognised as the same kind he used himself. Dev immediately challenged him to a maths race, which Cog won comprehensively, and then challenged him to a drawing race, which Dev won comprehensively, and then they spent half an hour teaching each other and both improved, which Dev said was the whole point."},
            {"page_number": 8, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "The thing Cog was worst at, he discovered, was understanding emotions. He could identify them — he had excellent facial recognition and he had studied body language extensively — but understanding why they happened and what they required in response was more difficult. Bea was patient about explaining. 'When someone is sad,' she said, 'you don't need to fix it. Sometimes you just stay.' Cog processed this. It went against his core programming, which was oriented toward problem-solving. 'Just stay?' he said. 'Yes,' said Bea. 'That's usually enough.' He practised staying. It turned out to require considerable effort but produced good results."},
            {"page_number": 9, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "The workshop owner noticed the change in Cog. The sorting was still perfect — impeccable, as always — but Cog seemed different in a way that was hard to define. More present, perhaps. More oriented toward the window. He came in one evening to find Bea sitting on the workshop floor, doing homework, while Cog sorted next to her in companionable quiet. They weren't talking. They were just there, in the same space, being comfortable with each other. The workshop owner watched them for a moment and then quietly backed out and went home. Some things, he understood, didn't need interfering with."},
            {"page_number": 10, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "One afternoon Cog told Bea something he had calculated but not said out loud: that before she had pressed her nose against his window, he had been running at approximately forty percent of his potential. Not mechanically — his sorting was always perfect. But in every other way. 'You were lonely,' Bea said. 'Yes,' said Cog. 'That is the correct word.' Bea thought about this. 'I was a bit lonely too,' she said. 'Even with people around. It's different when you find the right one.' Cog filed this carefully. He was going to need a much larger category labelled significant. THE END"},
