            {"page_number": 1, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "5e67f159-0c54-4ec5-ac37-b192137879db", "text": "On the first day at Heroic Heights School, the tradition was that every student received a cape. They came out of a wardrobe at the front of the hall one by one, drawn by some invisible process that matched cape to student: red for strength, blue for speed, gold for intelligence, silver for empathy, green for endurance. The hall rustled with the sound of children holding up their capes and feeling what they were. Nadia's was grey. She turned it over in her hands. It was a good quality grey — well made, neat stitching — but definitely, unmistakably, undeniably grey. 'Grey means undetermined,' said the teacher. 'Your power is still finding itself.'"},
            {"page_number": 2, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "3b56089c-6fb3-41dd-9b7a-138b48534d84", "text": "In Powers Development class, they practised. Around Nadia, children were discovering abilities with expressions ranging from terrified to euphoric. A boy named Theo lifted his desk with one finger and then put it down very carefully and didn't touch anything for twenty minutes. A girl called Suki ran to the wall and back so fast she appeared twice simultaneously. A boy named Reed got the answer to every question before the teacher finished asking it. Nadia sat at her desk and tried very hard to do something — anything. She concentrated. She focused. She achieved mild transparency in her left hand, which was not nothing, technically, but felt like nothing."},
            {"page_number": 3, "operation": "UPDATE", "page_id": "1ed9b03f-ae66-4036-9c91-b9b7c39004ce", "text": "The kitten challenge was set by Ms Marvel on the third day. A kitten had climbed the school's tallest oak tree and was sitting on the highest branch making the particular sound that means both 'help me' and 'I am also enjoying this attention' at once. Ms Marvel looked at her class. 'Solve it,' she said. Theo tried to lift the tree. He lifted it four inches before deciding this was inadvisable. Suki ran up the trunk — impressive — but the kitten retreated further. Reed knew exactly what to do but couldn't execute it. Nadia went to the canteen, found sardines, opened the tin, held it up. The kitten descended with dignified speed."},
            {"page_number": 4, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Nadia's cape shimmered at the edge of grey, the faintest silver beginning to show. Ms Marvel noticed but said nothing yet. In the classroom debrief, she asked each student what power they had used on the kitten problem. 'Strength,' said Theo. 'Speed,' said Suki. 'Analysis,' said Reed. Then she looked at Nadia. 'I just thought about what the kitten actually needed,' Nadia said. 'Rather than what I could do.' Ms Marvel wrote something on the board: PROBLEM DEFINITION. 'The most powerful skill in heroism,' she said, 'is understanding what problem you're actually solving before you try to solve it.'"},
            {"page_number": 5, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Hero History classes were Nadia's favourite. They studied heroes throughout time — not the ones with the most impressive powers, but the ones who had made the most difference. She was particularly interested in those who had solved problems that everyone else was solving wrong. A water engineer who ended a drought not by making it rain but by redesigning how water was stored. A negotiator who ended a conflict not by overpowering either side but by finding the one thing they both actually needed. 'These people had grey capes,' Nadia said to Ms Marvel after class. 'Almost certainly,' Ms Marvel agreed."},
            {"page_number": 6, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "The academy's annual Challenges week came in October. Each student faced a series of problems designed to test their specific powers. The others had straightforward tests: lift this, run here, answer this. Nadia's tests were different. She was given situations — a team that wasn't working, a resource that wasn't being used well, a conflict that had no obvious solution — and asked to improve them. She approached each one the same way: first understand exactly what the problem was, then find the simplest intervention that changed it. She solved every one. Her cape turned noticeably silver by the end of day three."},
            {"page_number": 7, "operation": "CREATE", "text": "Theo asked her to help him with something. He was enormously strong but kept accidentally breaking things — doors, chairs, equipment — because he didn't know his own power yet. 'What am I doing wrong?' he asked. Nadia watched him try to open a door and immediately saw it: he was applying strength uniformly instead of calibrating it. 'You're solving every problem at maximum,' she said. 'Try ten percent first and scale up.' Theo tried it. The door opened intact. He stared at his hands. 'How did you see that?' he asked. Nadia shrugged. 'I watched what you were actually doing.' It was the most useful thing anyone had said to him since he'd arrived."},
